    "created_at": "2023-01-10T17:37:49Z",
    "updated_at": "2023-01-10T17:37:49Z",
    "body": "> Releasing doesn't seem to have an exception opportunity and setting the state to closed doesn't either, so I'm not sure an early return is warranted as it wouldn't be testable. Unless releasing has some side effect I didn't spot.\r\n\r\nI guess it really depends on the implementation. For example: if the locking is implemented in a different process (process with the AccessHandle needs to message the \"LockManager\" process to release the \"lock\"); or if the AccessHandle is backed by a file descriptor, calling close twice could lead to file system error.\r\n\r\nI am not sure whether these errors should be observable by websites (whether we should throw exception on such failure), but it seems better to specify that these operations won't be executed twice no matter what the implementation is.\r\n",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
    "reactions": {
      "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/whatwg/fs/issues/comments/1377619226/reactions",
